![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

### Safe JSON Loading and Fail-Closed Behavior

The current implementation of `readJson` directly reads and parses JSON files without handling missing files or malformed JSON. If a critical validation file is missing or malformed, the script will crash with an unhandled exception rather than failing closed.

To ensure robust error handling and defensive programming, we should wrap the file read and JSON parse in a `try-catch` block, check for file existence, and return structured error diagnostics. This allows the validator to gracefully fail closed.

```javascript
function readJson(root, relPath) {
  try {
    const fullPath = path.join(root, relPath);
    if (!fs.existsSync(fullPath)) {
      return { error: "File not found: " + relPath, isMissing: true };
    }
    const content = fs.readFileSync(fullPath, 'utf8');
    return JSON.parse(content);
  } catch (error) {
    return { error: "Malformed JSON in " + relPath + ": " + error.message, isMalformed: true };
  }
}
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. When loading parsed files (such as JSON), validate the parsed file shape, record malformed entry diagnostics, skip bad entries, and pass those diagnostics into the package validator to ensure it fails closed.
</details>

